lesson: 144
title: The price of one thing from the money paid and the change
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: Price, quantity and cost belong together: cost = price × quantity, price = cost ÷ quantity, quantity = cost ÷ price.
explain: If you paid with more money than the things cost, the change came back: what the things cost is the money given minus the change.
explain: 3 pens bought with 50 dollars and 14 dollars change: they cost 50 - 14 = 36 dollars, so one pen costs 36 ÷ 3 = 12 dollars.
vars: a=name k=2..9 p=4..90 j=1..9
let: c = k * p
let: N = c - c % 10 + 10 * j
let: r = N - c
need: N <= 900
answer: p
exercise: {a} bought {k} notebooks, all at the same price. {a.He} paid with {N} dollars and got {r} dollars change. How much did one notebook cost?
exercise: {a} had {N} dollars. After buying {k} tickets at the same price, {a.he} had {r} dollars left. What was the price of one ticket?
exercise: Dad gave the shopkeeper {N} dollars for {k} equal bags of rice and got back {r} dollars. How much did one bag of rice cost?
exercise: {a} paid for {k} identical books with {N} dollars, and the change was {r} dollars. What was the price of one book?
exercise: A school paid {N} dollars for {k} footballs of one kind and received {r} dollars in change. How much did one football cost?
test: {r} dollars came back as change when {a} gave the cashier {N} dollars for {k} identical caps. What did one cap cost?
test: How much is one toy car, if {k} toy cars were paid for with {N} dollars and {r} dollars were returned?
test: {a} went to the market with {N} dollars, bought {k} kilograms of honey and came home with {r} dollars. What does one kilogram of honey cost?
exercise: How much did one notebook cost, if {a} bought {k} of them at the same price, paid with {N} dollars and got {r} dollars change?
exercise: {k} same-price pens, paid {N} dollars, change {r} dollars. Price of one pen?
exercise: In a shop in Chicago, {a} gave {N} dollars for {k} identical games and got back {r} dollars. What did one game cost?
exercise: {a} took {N} dollars to a fair in Texas and bought {k} rides that all cost the same. {a.He} came back with {r} dollars. How much was one ride?
exercise: Mum paid {N} dollars for {k} equal pizzas in Toronto and was given {r} dollars in change. What was the price of one pizza?
exercise: {a} had {N.words} dollars, bought {k.words} identical scarves and was left with {r} dollars. How much did each scarf cost?
exercise: A club in Sydney paid {N} dollars for {k} matching shirts and received {r} dollars back. What was one shirt's price?
exercise: {k} tickets of equal price were bought with {N} dollars, and {r} dollars were left over. How much did a ticket cost?
test: The cashier in Dublin handed {a} {r} dollars change from {N} dollars after {a.he} bought {k} identical puzzles. What is one puzzle's price?
test: What does one lamp cost, if {N} dollars were paid for {k} identical lamps and {r} dollars came back?
test: {a} spent part of {N} dollars on {k} equal bags of seeds in Oregon and still had {r} dollars. Find the cost of one bag.
test: From {N} dollars, {r} dollars were left after {k} same-priced umbrellas were bought in London. How much was each umbrella?
test: Dad gave {N} dollars at a shop in Auckland, got {r} dollars back, and took home {k} identical boxes of tea. What was the price of one box?
test: {k} football tickets in Ohio, all the same price, cost {N} dollars minus the {r} dollars of change. How much was a single ticket?
exercise: {a} bought {k} notebooks same price, paid {N} dollars, got {r} back. how much one
exercise: had {N} bucks, got {k} tickets same price, {r} left. price of one ticket?
exercise: {a} paid {N+10} — no wait, {N} dollars for {k} same bags of rice, got {r} back. one bag how much
exercise: my mate says one notebook's {N-r} dollars, {k} notebooks, paid {N}, {r} change. nah thats all of em. how much is one
test: {k} same pens, handed over {N} dollars, change {r}. one pen costs?
test: {a} paid {N} for {k} same cakes (and the queue was like 20 people lol), got {r} change. price of a cake
test: {a} reckons {k} toys, paid {N}, {r} change, so each toy is {p+r}. wrong yeah? how much really
exam: It is given that {a} purchased {k} notebooks at a uniform price, tendered {N} dollars in payment and received {r} dollars in change. Determine the price of a single notebook.
exam: Whereas {a} held {N} dollars and, following the purchase of {k} tickets of equal price, retained {r} dollars, ascertain the price of one ticket.
exam: The sum of {N} dollars was tendered to a shopkeeper for {k.words} bags of rice of equal price, and {r} dollars were returned. State the price, in dollars, of one bag.
exam: Compute the unit price of an article, given that {k} such articles, paid for with {N} dollars, occasioned change of {r} dollars.
exercise: {a} was economical with the truth about the shopping and said nothing about change. In fact {a.he} paid {N} dollars for {k} tickets at the same price and pocketed {r} dollars change. What was the price of one ticket?
exercise: When Gran passed away, Mum spent part of the {N} dollars she left on {k} equal flower wreaths, and {r} dollars were left. How much did one wreath cost?
test: {k} "pre-loved" jackets, same price each, paid {N} dollars, got {r} back. pre-loved my arse, they're just old. how much was one
